[116.801951, "o", "[approval] write docs/pip-diagnosis.md (+232 −0) [new file] — approved\r\n  ⚙ write docs/pip-diagnosis.md\r\n    ✓ write docs/pip-diagnosis.md (+232 −0)\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 20 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: wc -c docs/pip-diagnosis.md — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash wc -c docs/pip-diagnosis.md\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 21 tool call(s) so far\r\n  ⚙ task \r\n    ✓ task implementor — 21 tool call(s)\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 12 tool call(s) so far\r\n  [tools] 12 call(s) without changing a file — nudging to decide\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 12 tool call(s) so far\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 13 tool call(s) so far\r\n  ⚙ read docs/pip-diagnosis.md\r\n    ✓ read docs/pip-diagnosis.md — 232 lines\r\nDiagnosis is done and the evidence is on disk at `docs/pip-diagnosis.md`; I read it back in full (8,153 bytes).\r\n\r\n**First, the caveat that matters:** the harness runs in a Docker container (Debian 12, root, Python 3.13.1 built from python.org under `/usr/local`), and my file tools are sandboxe"]
[116.802105, "o", "d to this repository — so this is a diagnosis of the *container's* Python, not necessarily of your host. If your `pip` failure is on the host, the findings below are a strong lead but not proof.\r\n\r\n**What's actually wrong (confirmed, not guessed):** the `pip` Python package is missing from site-packages, while its metadata survives. `site-packages` contains `pip-24.3.1.dist-info/` but no `pip/` directory; `importlib.util.find_spec('pip')` returns `None`, so `python3 -m pip` fails with `No module named pip` and the `/usr/local/bin/pip3` script dies on `from pip._internal.cli.main import main`. The bundled wheel `ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl` is intact.\r\n\r\nRuled out with evidence: PEP 668 `EXTERNALLY-MANAGED` (no marker — this is a python.org build, not a distro one), permissions (running as root, writable site-packages), pip config (`config list -v`, `/etc/pip.conf`, `~/.pip/pip.conf`, XDG paths all absent), and environment (`PIP_REQUIRE_VIRTUALENV`, `PYTHONHOME/PYTHONPATH`, proxies, `VIR"]
[116.802148, "o", "TUAL_ENV` — none set).\r\n\r\n**Fix, to run by hand:**\r\n\r\n```bash\r\npython3 -m ensurepip --upgrade --default-pip\r\n```\r\n\r\nOne trap worth knowing: because `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` is still present, ensurepip can conclude \"Requirement already satisfied\" and do nothing. If it does, clear the orphan and retry:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\nrm -rf /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info\r\npython3 -m ensurepip --default-pip\r\n```\r\n\r\nI did not run either: my role here has no `bash`, and the fallback `get-pip.py` route needs the network, which `policy.toml` sets to `network = false`. If `pip install` is failing with something other than \"No module named pip\" on your host, `python3 -m pip install requests 2>&1` would settle it, and `docs/pip-diagnosis.md` ends with that note.\r\n\r\n[refusal] role=coordinator model=deepseek-v4.1-flash tools=13 surface=cb22b9617210\r\n"]
